Okay, Mike, whenever you're ready.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.700
I'll, no, I'll signal this though.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:36.320
Uh, so I, I was looking like pretty deep into this game because I think it's pretty obvious

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:37.440
because I've been talking the most.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:38.840
I, I kind of dig this game.

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:42.740
Okay. Uh, I'm not in love with it, but I dig it.

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:47.020
So I was trying to find something, you know, uh, about this game from the development side,

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:48.620
like, you know, a developer interview or something.

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:53.900
And I wasn't having much success with anything and I couldn't find anything from the directors

00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:59.040
or anything like that. But I did find, um, one of Capcom's employees, Yoichi Igawa.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:05.060
Um, he was talking about Trojan and, uh, this is the only developer thing I could find.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:07.500
So I'm going to read it. It's actually pretty cohesive, Sean.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:10.860
So, uh, it's not one of those like Nintendo rambles.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.780
We wanted to make a game where you push buttons.

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:15.460
Yeah. Oh, they got it.

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:19.960
They nailed it. Uh, we like to, we like to hide pairs of shoes in the sewer.

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:23.060
Huh? So here's, uh, here's Yoichi.

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:24.380
Here's what he had to say about it.

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:30.060
He goes, this was the first game I made when I joined Capcom and being my first game,

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:31.760
I hit the wall pretty quickly.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:35.080
There was one stage that gave me a ton of problems.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:39.060
And I remember getting its music stuck in my head over and over.

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:44.800
Even when I tried to sleep, the character that gave me the most trouble was the final boss,

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:46.760
the sword King Achilles.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.780
I put so much time into him trying to make him strong.

00:32:51.380 --> 00:32:53.760
There's one other memory I have of Trojan.

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:59.220
On the day of the deadline, I was riding my bike down the expressway to deliver the character

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:05.940
slash sprite ROMs, but I accidentally dropped the ROM chips and they were scattered across the road.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:12.300
I realized it right away and stopped my bike, but this was a very busy road.

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:18.140
And right before my eyes, I watched all those chips get crushed to pieces.

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:21.020
Oh, it was a cruel scene.

00:33:21.360 --> 00:33:25.880
Now I can laugh about it, but at the time my heart was sunk.

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:32.040
That sounds like, I mean, that wasn't really about the development of the game.

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:35.980
He just kind of, well, I think it was interesting that he was thinking about like, you know,

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:41.800
he himself was playing, like play testing this game as the designer, but also just like,

00:33:42.260 --> 00:33:45.620
well, not the designer, sorry, I forget who the designer was, but like, just like,

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:47.060
as one of the programmers in the game.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:52.160
And like, he's trying to like fight Achilles and making him as hard as possible while still beatable.

00:33:52.160 --> 00:33:55.220
And so I think that part was interesting that the ROM chip thing is just like tragic.

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:57.260
I know. It sounds like he's not really over it.

00:33:57.400 --> 00:33:59.000
Yeah. I feel bad for him.

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:08.290
I mean, it's probably like his most memorable moment from the whole development period,

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:15.340
you know? Yeah. And I think it's okay because he is now the executive corporate officer and director of Capcom Co.

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:18.000
Oh, good for you. So he's doing okay.

00:34:18.200 --> 00:34:19.740
He's doing just fine. That's probably a nice job.

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:24.640
Yeah. It is probably a nice job considering the CEO of Capcom.

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:26.740
I'm just, oh, no, no, sorry.

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:30.200
No, we don't have a CEO of Capcom position here.

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:31.740
I was trying to find out compensations.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.700
You're trying to figure out how much money he makes.

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:37.960
Yeah. I was trying to figure out how much money he makes as this guy, but we can't really get

00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:46.200
that. But apparently if you're the CEO of a video game company in Japan, you can see like somewhere

00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:48.760
of like 100 to 400 million yen.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:52.780
So Mike is just idly Wikipedia-ing things over here.

00:34:53.120 --> 00:34:53.920
How much is that?

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:56.360
How much is yen? I was trying to actually think that out too.

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:57.760
Is it close to like a thousand to one?

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:03.780
Let's find out. A 400 million yen is $3 million.

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:05.800
Yeah. So that's actually realistic.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:07.960
Thank goodness. I was like, this sounds stupid.

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:11.180
I was like, he makes 400 million yen.

00:35:11.700 --> 00:35:13.140
It's like Michael Jordan's salary.

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:14.640
You know, it's like you would think, but it's not.

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:15.700
It's not. That's what I'm saying.

00:35:15.700 --> 00:35:19.840
Like when you hear 400 million yen, you would think it's one of those things where it's

00:35:19.840 --> 00:35:24.820
like, while he sleeps, a thousand people don't even make as much money as him.

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:26.420
Like that's not true though.

00:35:26.920 --> 00:35:28.260
Video game designers, who knew?

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:29.200
They don't make that much money.

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:50.420
Who knew? No, they do. No, they do.

00:35:50.420 --> 00:35:53.140
No, they do.

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:08.140
on is the, you know, kind of the weird addition, other than King Sheik, King Shriek, the weird

